00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.870
PROFESSOR: All
right, so we're going

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.290
to do a basic three-point
lighting setup.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.450
This is one of the kits I use.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:31.770
This is a compact, one-case
lighting kit as I call it.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.190
It's got a few-- we're going
to go over what's in it here.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.750
Here's one very important
item in the kit.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.060
It has everything
to do with lighting.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.180
Transparent microbubble powder.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:51.560
Sometimes, your subject
will be a little too shiny

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.400
under the lights, and
this transparent base

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.740
will get rid of the
shine without changing

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.540
the color of their complexion.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.960
And if you use the
transparent powder,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.680
always use a brand
new makeup pad

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.600
or wedge because you don't
want to cause problems.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.077
But that's an essential
item in the kit.

00:01:16.077 --> 00:01:17.910
This is a grid, which
we'll see in a moment.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.500
We'll pull that out.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.180
A couple of extension cords.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.150
Very useful items to have.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.460
This is the Lowel Rifa.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.170
What's really nice
about the Rifa

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.590
is that it's very
compact, soft light that

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:37.320
folds up like an umbrella.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.080
And very easy to pop open.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.093
And it opens up like that.

00:01:51.093 --> 00:01:52.635
And we will revisit
this in a moment.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.340
Extension cords.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.840
And if my extension cord doesn't
have multiple taps on the end,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.880
I always attach a cube
tap because you never

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.255
know when you're going to need
to plug in something else.

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.730
This is the Lowel Tota.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.250
Basic open face light.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.690
Comes with a safety screen.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.300
And you should always
use it with the screen.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.040
Quartz halogen lamps.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.420
When they shatter, you
don't want hot glass

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.470
anywhere near living things.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:30.070
So if you use the
Lowel Tota Light,

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:32.070
you definitely want to
make sure that the safety

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.330
screen is always on it.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:38.700
The new generation V light
encapsulates the lamp inside

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.680
of a glass case for safety.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.650
But that's the older style.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.230
Here's a Fresnel instrument.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.350
There is your
scalloped glass lens.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:57.290
And all Fresnel instruments
have a flood to the right spot,

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:58.700
to the left flood.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.420
The cleanest, most even light
source comes in flood mode.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.350
And we'll see how that
floods and spots in a moment.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:09.270
Barn doors are usually included.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:13.070
And we'll put these
on the Fresnel.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:17.150
And the barn doors are used
for controlling the spill.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.550
And they get their
name because they

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.190
seem to look like barn doors.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.910
Of course, I've
never seen a barn

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:26.570
that's shaped round like that.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:34.230
But usually travel with
a second extension cord.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.200
This one's green because
I bought it at 75% off

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.760
in an after Christmas sale.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.190
That's the best time
to buy extension cords.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.260
For some reason the
green ones become

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.110
much cheaper than the
other colored ones the day

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:46.950
after Christmas.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.500
Manfrotto makes a
variety of light stands.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.010
These are nice, lightweight
aluminum stands,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:04.170
which are very handy for
when you're traveling.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.360
And we will set
those up in a moment.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.550
When you set up the
stands, you should always

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:18.029
make sure that the bottoms
are as wide out as they

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:20.610
go for maximum stability.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:38.880
And here's the umbrella that
goes with the Tota-light, which

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.810
we will be using today as well.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:41.655
So this attaches.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:52.650
The Tota-light has an accessory,
has an accessory connection

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:53.400
thing--

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.590
that's a technical term, thing--

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.650
that it attaches to
the light, and that

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.550
gives us a nice, soft light,
which we'll see in a moment.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.980
Here's the quarter blue
gel I was talking about.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.760
So for example, if I
hold this over the lens

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.120
and we set the white
balance, we can actually

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:22.050
increase the warmth
of the world.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.200
So we can fool the
white balance circuit

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.670
into thinking that the
world is more blue.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.490
And then we pull
it, pull it away.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:34.460
So this eighth blue gel is
very handy dandy thing to have.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.370
Also in the kit we
have the Siemens stars

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:40.160
for focusing, for
precise focusing.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.900
These are really
easy to focus on.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.270
And this is also a gray card,
and on the opposite side

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:48.793
a white card for balancing.

00:05:48.793 --> 00:05:50.210
You never know
when you might have

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.420
trouble finding an 8 and 1/2
by 11 piece of white paper.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.110
So I always keep the white
card in the kit as well.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.430
We talked about gloves.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:04.070
These will keep you
from burning your hands.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:08.330
And very important, always
have a set of spare lamps.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.310
Lamps have a
tendency to burn out

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:12.440
in the most inopportune moment.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:17.105
So I always keep spare lamps
in a nice little cushioned box.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:24.990
Sometimes when you're shooting
outdoors, all you need

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:28.500
is a reflector to
fill in the shadows.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.710
This wonderful device
called the Flexfill,

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.965
which opens up like this.

00:06:34.965 --> 00:06:36.340
They come in
different varieties.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:39.840
I like the matte silver
on one side and the matte

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.710
white on the other.

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:45.590
So if we need to
fill in the shadows

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:50.990
with a very, very kind of spunky
light or a very, very subtle

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:54.320
light, we have those two sides.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:57.930
You store them away by
giving them a little twist

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:02.480
and throwing them
back into the case.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.230
You can get accessories
that will hold the Flexfill

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:08.090
and you can attach
it to a light stand.

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.010
I have one of those
in my larger kit.

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:16.080
But that usually doesn't
travel in the smaller kit.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:24.450
And sometimes I want to color,
I want to color the light.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:30.420
I keep a little file here with
a variety of colored gels--

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:35.310
blues for cooling things
off, orange and pink gels

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:36.780
for warming things up.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.315
I actually put these in
the order of the rainbow.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:49.410
And of course, and I
also have a bunch of--

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:53.460
I keep the orange and the blues
for warming up and cooling

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:58.930
lights in their own set,
along with some diffusion.

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:00.970
So if I want to diffuse
the light, for example,

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:04.060
this is standard 216
or full diffusion.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:06.640
You put this over light
and it'll really soften it.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.980
So we're going to look
at some diffusion today.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.380
And this is actually
a half blue gel

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:19.650
that is actually specifically
cut for use with the Rifa-lite.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:23.310
The blue gel is used to
bring the warm tungsten light

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:26.310
of the Rifa closer to daylight.

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.830
I use the half blue
instead of the full blue

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.920
because the full blue cuts the
light way too much, kind of

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.720
like, is it 2 or
2 and 1/2 stops?

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:39.020
The full blue cuts the
light a little too much.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:42.850
The half blue still gives
me some light intensity

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:43.840
to work with.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.700
So often I will
split the difference

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.030
in the white balance.

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:50.860
So I keep one of
these specifically cut

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:54.008
and sized for the
Rifa-lite so it

00:08:54.008 --> 00:08:55.300
can be attached really quickly.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:01.970
Now it's very
important-- the Rifa-lite

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:06.530
has a bare, incandescent lamp.

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:11.030
It's very important that
you don't touch the lamp.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.520
You don't want to get
finger oils on it.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:16.533
They can cause the
fingerprint area

00:09:16.533 --> 00:09:18.950
to be at a different temperature
than the rest of the lamp

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.360
and the glass could crack.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.490
So you want to be very careful.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:28.630
Never operate the Rifa-lite
without the diffusion cover.

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:30.040
So here's the low Rifa.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:33.490
And there is an optional
accessory, the offset arm,

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:34.630
which is really important.

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:38.650
If you're mounting this on a
relatively lightweight stand,

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:40.580
it's going to tip
over really easily.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.390
But with the offset
arm, the weight

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:45.320
is balanced evenly
on a light stand.

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:49.270
So this offset arm is a
pretty important accessory.

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:55.540
Never use the light
without the diffusion cover

00:09:55.540 --> 00:10:00.820
because this not only is
providing the diffused light,

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:04.990
but it's also covering up
the quartz halogen lamp,

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:10.780
because you never want to
have a bare lamp in front

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:11.650
of your talent.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:18.440
And this kind of
attaches with Velcro.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:24.960
Here we go.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.590
So there is our soft light.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:29.940
It comes with a short cord.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:35.090
And so we need the, comes with
its own little extension cord

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:38.030
with a standard IEC connector.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.490
And there is our soft key.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:53.550
And Zack, could you plug
that into the power strip?

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:04.700
All right.

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:08.900
So now we have a
lovely soft light,

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:12.538
which we'll use as our key.

00:11:12.538 --> 00:11:14.330
So Chris, are you ready
for your interview?

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:14.765
CHRIS BOEBEL: I am.

00:11:14.765 --> 00:11:15.723
DAVID TAMES: All right.

00:11:15.723 --> 00:11:17.630
So we're going to
interview Chris today.

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:22.100
And here is Zack, is going
to be operating camera.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:26.300
And we're going to
start with our Rifa-lite

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.920
as our key light on Chris.

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:38.205
CHRIS BOEBEL: Glasses
on or off, David?

00:11:38.205 --> 00:11:39.205
DAVID TAMES: Glasses on.

00:11:39.205 --> 00:11:40.120
CHRIS BOEBEL: OK.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.500
DAVID TAMES: It gives us
more fun to work with.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:43.440
Right?

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:51.120
So now, right now we're
using the Rifa as our key,

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.150
and we have a lot of overhead
lights in this classroom.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:58.770
I'm going to turn off the
ambient light for now.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:04.020
So this is a single key light,
no fill, no background light,

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:05.640
no backlight.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:06.315
Pretty dramatic.

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:13.640
What we would call a
very high lighting ratio,

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:18.710
probably something
worthy of film noir.

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:22.010
It may or may not be
appropriate for our piece,

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:24.330
but that is our starting point.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:27.770
We typically set our key
light, our primary light source

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:30.050
and determine what
we want it to do.

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:34.440
Notice how we get a
very different quality

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:38.790
if I raise the key
light a little bit.

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:48.950
And if I move it off to the
side versus moving it more

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:58.780
towards the front, this is
a little more glamorous.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:01.120
Now there's no dramatic shadow.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.450
It's almost like an overhead
towards the front key light.

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:08.800
So notice how as we
bring it to the side

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:17.680
and bring it down a little bit,
we have a much more dramatic

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.860
arrangement.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:22.510
So now what we want
to do is we want

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:28.330
to fill the shadows that are
being created by the key light

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:31.570
and maybe bring a back
light or a background light

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:32.410
into the picture.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:54.530
For the fill light, I think
we'll use our Tota-light

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:55.310
with an umbrella.

00:14:06.507 --> 00:14:08.090
What's handy about
all these lowlights

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:14.450
is they use the same
connector, same cable style.

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:18.980
It's nice that all the
lowlights have pretty much

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:21.590
the same connector
and wiring system.

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:22.790
They're interchangeable.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:28.370
And they wrap easily into a case
and can be stored very portably

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:30.710
because the cords separate out.

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:36.440
So we're going to use
this Tota as our fill.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:42.050
And notice what's going on
here, that now our situation is

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:44.300
a little less dramatic.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:50.210
And as I bring
the fill light in,

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:53.100
I'm reducing the contrast ratio.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.710
So when we talk
about contrast ratio,

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:58.430
we're talking about the
ratio between the fill light

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:03.560
and key, the fill and key
together, and just the fill.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:12.180
So notice that in this
case, the fill and the key

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.190
are very similar in intensity.

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:20.050
They're kind of, the
ratio is almost 1 to 1,

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:21.750
might be closer to 1 to 2.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:24.300
So as I bring my key--

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:27.060
sorry, as I bring
my fill light back,

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:30.540
I'm increasing
the lighting ratio

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:37.530
and providing a more
dramatic experience.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:39.180
I'm providing a more
dramatic lighting

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:40.650
situation for the subject.

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:46.337
Now since this is, we might
split the difference there

00:15:46.337 --> 00:15:47.670
and go with something like that.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:48.570
How does that look, Zack?

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:49.530
GUEST SPEAKER: It looks good.

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:50.488
DAVID TAMES: All right.

00:15:50.488 --> 00:15:52.980
So now, we have a choice.

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:57.720
We we can give Chris a backlight
for a more dramatic theatrical

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:02.490
look, or we can simply
use our third light source

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:05.400
as a background light.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:09.750
But I think what we'll do now is
let's see what a backlight does

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:10.860
for Chris.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:12.990
So we're going to use
this 300 Watt Fresnel

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.480
as a backlight on Chris.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:18.630
We're going to set
it up over here.

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:21.610
Sometimes I do it
opposite the key.

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:24.750
A lot of times, it
depends on my mood,

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:28.845
what seems to make sense for the
particular scene in question.

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:41.550
Notice that we're getting
light directly into the camera.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:45.840
You can see-- you're
getting flares, Jared?

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:50.055
So we're getting flares in
probably both camera lenses.

00:16:50.055 --> 00:16:51.180
CHRIS BOEBEL: Both cameras.

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:54.780
DAVID TAMES: Notice how by
adjusting the barn doors,

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:59.010
I can reduce that lens flare.

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:01.860
So what's nice about a
Fresnel instrument is

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:05.609
the barn doors actually work
because the light beams are

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:10.270
so straight, they're
easy to control.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:15.599
So what we've done here is we've
provided a backlight on Chris

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:22.260
and we've used the barn doors
to reduce the spill directly

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:25.560
into the camera lens to avoid
that flare that you saw.

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:28.530
Now, this is looking kind of
dramatic, wouldn't you say?

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:38.850
Now, we've got these great
little things called scrims,

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:42.420
which we can pop into
our Fresnel instrument

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:44.220
and reduce the intensity.

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:50.750
CHRIS BOEBEL: A scrim is
just a wire screen that

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:53.360
fits in front of the
lamp and cuts out,

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.748
literally just cuts down how
much light comes out of it.

00:17:55.748 --> 00:17:57.290
DAVID TAMES: So
notice I have brought

00:17:57.290 --> 00:18:02.690
down the intensity
of the backlight

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:05.300
without having to move it
and change its position

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:06.620
and quality.

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:10.550
I've just used the scrims
to reduce the intensity.

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:13.280
So that's another--
most Fresnel instruments

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:17.870
have slots for single
or double scrims.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:21.200
A double scrim basically cuts
the light one stop, cuts it

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:27.050
in half, or you can cut it
less with a single scrim.

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.640
CHRIS BOEBEL: The other nice
thing about a scrim, if just--

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:30.890
DAVID TAMES: Those are halves.

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:32.810
CHRIS BOEBEL: So
this also allows

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:36.050
you to cut the bottom
part or the top part.

00:18:36.050 --> 00:18:38.630
You can actually adjust where
the scrim goes in the light

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:43.610
and have some control over
which part, up or down,

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:46.220
side left or right is cut more.

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:46.970
DAVID TAMES: Yeah.

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:52.490
So there's single and
doubles, and there's halves,

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:54.740
which are physically half.

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:56.822
So sometimes you
want half the beam

00:18:56.822 --> 00:18:58.280
to be brighter than
the other half.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:01.400
Now sometimes I
like my backlights

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:04.940
to be a slightly different
color than my key lights.

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:11.930
So I'm going to put a blue
gel, attaching it to the barn

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:17.840
doors with clothespins,
also known as C-47s.

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:21.320
And notice what's
going on now is

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:25.980
we've cooled off a little bit.

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.490
And I might want to
remove those scrims now.

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:35.570
By the way, scrims
can get very hot.

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:38.210
You don't want to pull them
out with your bare hands.

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:41.090
Sometimes people will
take a pair of clothespins

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:44.300
and hook it up
backwards and you end up

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:46.880
with what's called
a scrim puller.

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:50.690
So notice now that my
backlight is a little bluish

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:52.970
and it's a little intense.

00:19:52.970 --> 00:19:56.070
We might want to
tweak this further.

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:59.300
But for the sake of this demo--

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:00.710
how are the glasses looking?

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:04.790
Another issue that we deal
with is reflections in glasses.

00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:06.500
So there's several
things we can do

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:08.300
about reflections in glasses.

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:13.730
One thing we can do is we can
move the position of our key.

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:18.020
Crossing.

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:20.510
All right.

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:23.510
Sometimes that can help.

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:31.920
We have a tricky situation here
with both the fill and the key.

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:37.440
But notice now
we've reduced, we've

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:39.570
eliminated that
second reflection.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:41.640
We do have, we still
have a little bit

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:45.900
of reflection in the glasses,
but as Chris talks and moves

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:50.010
his head a little bit,
you want to watch out.

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:53.520
Some people simply say
take your glasses off.

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:57.730
I like to do a little bit of
extra work and ask people,

00:20:57.730 --> 00:20:59.340
do you normally
wear glasses or not?

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:01.830
If they take them off
because they'd only wear them

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:03.180
some of the times, fine.

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:05.340
If they usually
wear their glasses,

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:08.160
I'll spend a little extra
time tweaking the lights

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:13.312
and get it to look nice so
that I'm photographing people

00:21:13.312 --> 00:21:14.520
the way they normally appear.

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:22.260
Sometimes changing the position
by tilting the legs up or down

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:25.890
a little bit, sometimes a
little bit of adjustment

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:29.160
can help with the
reflection in glasses.

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:33.160
It's a little tricky,
but it can work.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:35.400
So this is a pretty
simple, straightforward

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:38.610
three-point-- key, fill, back.

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:41.040
We could have added
some accent lights

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:42.390
for details in the background.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:45.095
We could have added a background
light to light our background.

00:21:45.095 --> 00:21:47.220
But we're just going to
keep it simple for the sake

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:48.940
of this demo.

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:50.280
So notice what happens now.

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:54.540
So here's our backlight.

00:21:57.180 --> 00:21:57.990
Here's our fill.

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:03.090
Here's our key light.

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:10.740
We'll fill in the shadows
with our fill light,

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:16.920
and we'll provide a little bit
of drama or theatrical essence

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:19.980
with our backlight and give
a little bit of separation

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:24.660
between foreground
and background.

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:25.410
One more thing.

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:28.410
The thing about soft
lights is they spill a lot.

00:22:28.410 --> 00:22:30.840
So notice how our
key is spilling

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:33.030
all over the background.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.340
There's one more
accessory that I

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:39.900
carry in the light kit
along with the soft light,

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:41.760
and that's called a grid.

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:44.130
And what this grid
will do is it will

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:45.960
reduce the spill of the light.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:51.660
And it's available in different
size grids for different angles

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:58.400
of allowing different beat.

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:01.460
It's available in
different size grids,

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:08.480
providing different
amounts of spill reduction.

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:30.670
So now with the grid,
using the grid in our key,

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:34.270
you will notice it does
reduce the intensity

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:37.160
of the key light a bit.

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:45.330
However, notice, and it might
be hard to see from this camera,

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:47.520
but we've dramatically
reduced the amount of spill

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:48.930
on the background.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:51.690
We've eliminated
the background spill

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:53.850
and the light is
primarily on the subject.

00:23:56.770 --> 00:23:58.360
And since the
intensity of our key

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:00.435
came down, because
the grid does reduce

00:24:00.435 --> 00:24:01.810
the intensity of
the light a bit,

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:08.150
I might want to pull my fill
light back a little bit.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:12.403
So now we have a somewhat
dramatic interview setup.

00:24:12.403 --> 00:24:15.070
We're still getting a little bit
of reflection with the glasses.

00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:17.665
That's something that
we would need to tweak.

00:24:17.665 --> 00:24:19.790
One more thing I might do
in a situation like this,

00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:22.120
but we're going to
skip it for brevity,

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:25.810
is I might add an additional
light unit in order

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:30.250
to provide some definition
in the background.

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:31.330
One last thing.

00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:43.470
By the way, instead
of using a fill light,

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:46.440
I could have used the Flexfill.

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:50.970
So notice how here,
grabbing some of the light

00:24:50.970 --> 00:24:55.380
from the key light,
I can actually

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:58.520
use the silver side
of the Flexfill

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:04.555
to fill in the shadows.

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:12.680
Though sometimes in
an interview setup

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:17.300
it might be better
to have a light unit

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:20.210
farther away from the
subject than the Flexfill.

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:22.620
But the Flexfill
is kind of handy

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:24.620
and it doesn't have to
be plugged into the wall.

00:25:28.297 --> 00:25:30.755
All right, one last thing I
want to show is a scrim puller.

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:35.460
So Fresnel instruments
usually allow

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:38.910
you to pop in scrims
so that you can

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:42.030
adjust the intensity of the
light once it's in place.

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:45.550
Because the scrims are
metal, they get really hot.

00:25:45.550 --> 00:25:50.550
So if you reassemble a
pair of clothespins kind

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:53.490
of with the clips
backwards, you can actually

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:55.170
create what's called
a scrim puller.

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:58.365
And you want to use those
for popping out the scrims

00:25:58.365 --> 00:26:00.240
because they can get
really hot and you don't

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:02.178
want to burn your fingers.

00:26:02.178 --> 00:26:03.720
CHRIS BOEBEL: So
what we've done here

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:05.910
is a pretty traditional,
straightforward

00:26:05.910 --> 00:26:07.800
three-point lighting
setup for an interview,

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:09.633
and I just wanted to
make a couple of points

00:26:09.633 --> 00:26:12.600
about what other things
to think about if this

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.430
were, in fact, an interview.

00:26:14.430 --> 00:26:16.410
So David has set me up here.

00:26:16.410 --> 00:26:21.450
I'm the interview subject, he's
the interviewer, presumably.

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:24.180
In a traditional
interview situation,

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:26.910
I would be looking
just off-camera,

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:28.410
either to the left or the right.

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:31.530
And what we have set up here
with the main on this side

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:34.050
is essentially
probably something

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:36.480
that would favor me looking
to the left of the camera.

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:40.780
So David, if you stand where
the interviewer would be--

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:42.447
DAVID TAMES: Hey there.

00:26:42.447 --> 00:26:44.280
CHRIS BOEBEL: --and I'm
talking to you here,

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:47.250
you see that I'm just looking
a little bit off-camera.

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:48.660
Maybe I'm looking here.

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:52.200
This is the side that is the
main side of the lighting.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:53.760
It's more brightly lit.

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:55.440
And then the other
thing would be

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:59.070
thinking about framing in
relation to the lighting.

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:01.440
I don't know what kind
of frame you have there

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:03.960
since I'm not the
director, but you

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:07.350
might want to think about
whether this kind of lighting

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:09.570
and this kind of
frame works well

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:13.470
in both a fairly close shot,
depending on the style you're

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.570
going for, and a wider
shot, because you

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:18.570
may want to vary the frame
size during the course

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:20.430
of the interview.

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:22.710
When David asked me the
very emotional question

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:27.340
about my childhood, this is
the 60 Minutes interview.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:28.298
You'll be much tighter.

00:27:28.298 --> 00:27:29.798
DAVID TAMES: You
want to zoom in for

00:27:29.798 --> 00:27:31.033
that perfect little teardrop.

00:27:31.033 --> 00:27:31.950
CHRIS BOEBEL: Exactly.

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:34.355
Yes, the teardrop quivering
on just the edge of the--

00:27:34.355 --> 00:27:35.730
DAVID TAMES: Little
did we know--

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:36.240
CHRIS BOEBEL: --the eye.

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:38.250
DAVID TAMES: --there was a cut
and a makeup person put that

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:39.210
there with an eye dropper.

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:40.130
CHRIS BOEBEL: [LAUGHS]

00:27:40.130 --> 00:27:41.510
DAVID TAMES: [LAUGHS]

00:27:41.510 --> 00:27:43.050
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yes,
when I was scared

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:45.730
by a video camera
at age four and it

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:46.980
changed the course of my life.

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:49.830
But anyway, so I just
wanted to point that out

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:52.420
that this could be an actual
live interview situation

00:27:52.420 --> 00:27:54.990
and there would be things that
we would be thinking about,

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:59.610
both in terms of framing
and position of the subject,

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:01.230
and the lighting impacts that.

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:02.460
DAVID TAMES: Right.

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:06.000
Another thing I'd like
to add to that is I

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:08.310
typically do not do
all of this setup

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:10.860
with the interview
subject standing in.

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:11.610
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yes.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:14.040
DAVID TAMES: So
typically, I work

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:17.430
in a two-person
team and the person

00:28:17.430 --> 00:28:20.100
doing the interview will hang
out with the interview subject

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:22.770
and kind of chat with them
and kind of warm things up,

00:28:22.770 --> 00:28:26.640
and do that away from
all the technical setup

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:29.190
and I will do the
technical setup

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:33.780
and then only do the very last
minute tweaking and adjustments

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:36.390
with the subject sitting under
the lights, because it could

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:39.390
take 30 minutes to set
everything up really carefully,

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:41.970
sound, lighting, et
cetera, and you don't want

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:43.943
to put people through that.

00:28:43.943 --> 00:28:46.110
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, I mean,
we have a separate class

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:47.875
on interview
techniques and things

00:28:47.875 --> 00:28:49.500
to think about when
doing an interview.

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:53.580
But the comfort and relaxed
state of your subject

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:55.320
is obviously really,
really crucial.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.820
And putting them through what
I just went through, you know,

00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:00.840
I'm patient because I don't
mind being a guinea pig here,

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.798
but putting them through
that is not a good way

00:29:02.798 --> 00:29:05.257
to begin a relationship when
you're going to be asking them

00:29:05.257 --> 00:29:05.825
questions.

00:29:05.825 --> 00:29:07.200
Similarly, another
point I wanted

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:10.783
to make about the lighting
is you want to, obviously,

00:29:10.783 --> 00:29:12.450
make things look good
and put the lights

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:15.480
where they need to be in order
to get a good image and a look

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:17.110
that works for you.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:19.900
But you also want to think about
the comfort of your subject.

00:29:19.900 --> 00:29:23.370
So again, having
something glaring directly

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:26.850
in their eyes, a very powerful
light can be a problem.

00:29:26.850 --> 00:29:28.950
And raising the
light a little bit

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:31.770
higher so that it's not as--

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:33.810
see, right there, that
makes a huge difference.

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:34.602
DAVID TAMES: Right.

00:29:34.602 --> 00:29:36.780
In fact, usually--
and that's actually

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:39.780
another reason why I prefer
the [? sidey ?] light.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:43.200
It's not just about
being dramatic and kind

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:46.740
of following in the Rembrandt
tradition, but a lot of it

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:50.482
is also driven by not having
the light glare directly

00:29:50.482 --> 00:29:51.190
into the subject.

00:29:51.190 --> 00:29:53.400
CHRIS BOEBEL: So now if
I'm talking to David back

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:55.590
in the interviewer
position, I can see him,

00:29:55.590 --> 00:29:57.390
I'm not looking into the light.

00:29:57.390 --> 00:29:59.850
It's just much more
comfortable for me.

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:03.900
There's also another thing that
people often do called comfort

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:07.050
lights, which basically would
be another small light that

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:08.490
actually isn't
lighting me at all,

00:30:08.490 --> 00:30:10.232
but is a very low
power light that's

00:30:10.232 --> 00:30:11.940
lighting David or
lighting the background

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:14.790
so that I'm not just
staring into this black well

00:30:14.790 --> 00:30:17.340
with these bright
lights pointing at me.

00:30:17.340 --> 00:30:19.290
I actually can see
who I'm talking to,

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:21.627
I can see the environment
just a little bit.

00:30:21.627 --> 00:30:23.460
And we keep them very
low and very low power

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.440
so that they don't
actually affect the image,

00:30:25.440 --> 00:30:29.160
but it just adds to the
comfort level of the person.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:31.990
But yeah, David also made an
extremely good and important

00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:36.717
point that when you're
working doing an interview,

00:30:36.717 --> 00:30:38.800
particularly if it is kind
of a sensitive subject,

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:44.470
having two people is really,
really important not just

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:47.050
to schlep the equipment,
but because one person can

00:30:47.050 --> 00:30:49.570
be developing a rapport
with the interview subject

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:52.090
while the other person is
handling all of this stuff.

00:30:52.090 --> 00:30:54.760
And it gets really,
really difficult

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:56.908
sometimes if you put
somebody through,

00:30:56.908 --> 00:30:59.200
especially someone who's not
really used to doing this,

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:03.910
through this whole experience
of the tweaking and the lighting

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:06.820
setup and glaring lights in
their eyes, and then you say,

00:31:06.820 --> 00:31:08.570
Go, ready, start.

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:12.250
And that can be a very
off-putting experience.

00:31:12.250 --> 00:31:13.390
DAVID TAMES: Yeah.

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:17.050
And one last thing is we've done
this pretty dramatic, almost

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:20.360
theatrical setup for
this particular demo.

00:31:20.360 --> 00:31:23.710
But if I was interviewing Chris
about teaching this class,

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:26.710
we would probably
find a nice window,

00:31:26.710 --> 00:31:29.980
maybe facing north or south so
we don't get direct sunlight

00:31:29.980 --> 00:31:31.540
in the middle of
the interview, we'd

00:31:31.540 --> 00:31:35.620
sit by that window and some
soft light, and we'd talk there,

00:31:35.620 --> 00:31:39.340
and I'd probably never pull
a lighting instrument out

00:31:39.340 --> 00:31:40.210
of the kit.

00:31:40.210 --> 00:31:43.690
So start with Mother Nature,
see if the natural light works

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:47.598
for you, and if not, you can
always work artificially.

00:31:47.598 --> 00:31:50.140
CHRIS BOEBEL: And remember to
apply the principles that we've

00:31:50.140 --> 00:31:53.740
been talking about, both in the
lecture part and in this demo,

00:31:53.740 --> 00:31:58.185
to non-lighting
instrument situations.

00:31:58.185 --> 00:32:00.310
A little bit of backlight
adds a lot of definition.

00:32:00.310 --> 00:32:02.200
Well, there are other
ways to get backlight

00:32:02.200 --> 00:32:03.700
than setting up a light.

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:05.828
DAVID TAMES: Maybe we
should do one last setup--

00:32:05.828 --> 00:32:06.620
CHRIS BOEBEL: Sure.

00:32:06.620 --> 00:32:09.760
DAVID TAMES: --using the natural
lights available in this room.

00:32:09.760 --> 00:32:12.295
CHRIS BOEBEL: The camera
currently is not correctly

00:32:12.295 --> 00:32:12.920
white balanced.

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:14.378
That's why everything
looks orange.

00:32:14.378 --> 00:32:16.435
And we're going to do a
white balance for you.

00:32:16.435 --> 00:32:17.935
DAVID TAMES: So
here's a white card.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:26.690
All right.

00:32:26.690 --> 00:32:29.720
So here, let's white
balance the taking camera.

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:33.410
Zack is going to white
balance the Sony a1.

00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:36.555
P menu and then white balance.

00:32:36.555 --> 00:32:37.805
And then there's the one push.

00:32:50.340 --> 00:32:50.850
All right.

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:55.350
So now this is what Chris looks
like with a technically correct

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:57.790
white balance.

00:32:57.790 --> 00:32:58.920
So now let's do that again.

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:00.990
Chris, if you could
hold up the white card.

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:04.410
Let's do that again and
hold an eighth blue filter

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:05.160
over the lens.

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:06.993
We're going to fool the
camera into thinking

00:33:06.993 --> 00:33:08.818
there's more blue in the world.

00:33:08.818 --> 00:33:10.860
Now we're going to do the
one push white balance.

00:33:17.527 --> 00:33:18.360
DAVID TAMES: Got it?

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:21.600
And now we pull away
the blue, and Chris

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:23.150
looks a little warmer.

00:33:23.150 --> 00:33:24.660
A teeny bit warmer.

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:25.740
Just a tad.

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:29.460
It's like adding a little bit of
basil to your spaghetti sauce.

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:32.430
Slightly more flavorful.

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:36.060
Now this is beautiful
window light.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:38.610
We're not getting direct
sun shining into the room.

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:42.690
So it's all beautiful,
soft sky light.

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:48.660
And from this camera angle
that Zack is shooting from,

00:33:48.660 --> 00:33:49.920
looks reasonably good.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:52.980
It's a bit dramatic
because remember

00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:54.075
that inverse square law.

00:33:54.075 --> 00:33:55.960
It's kind of hard
to get away from it.

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:58.380
The light falls
off pretty quickly.

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:02.280
But at this angle, Chris, if
you look at me, from this angle

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:07.410
we're getting a pretty
nice wrap around his face.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:10.830
Clearly, we have a bright side
and a darker side of the face,

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:12.190
but it looks very natural.

00:34:12.190 --> 00:34:17.429
It's kind of hard to beat really
nice, Vermeer-like soft window

00:34:17.429 --> 00:34:17.945
light.

00:34:17.945 --> 00:34:19.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
of course, just

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:21.212
as you can change the
light or the position

00:34:21.212 --> 00:34:22.920
of the light in the
other setup, here you

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:24.628
could change the
position of the subject.

00:34:24.628 --> 00:34:27.960
So you could have me move, and
if you could just pan with me,

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:28.710
move this way.

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:30.168
DAVID TAMES: If I
move Chris back--

00:34:30.168 --> 00:34:32.730
CHRIS BOEBEL: And as I
move here, it gets flatter.

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:34.570
I'm lit from the front.

00:34:34.570 --> 00:34:37.110
DAVID TAMES: See,
notice how flat that is?

00:34:37.110 --> 00:34:38.820
But now, Chris, if
you move right here

00:34:38.820 --> 00:34:42.300
and look straight into the
camera and look over here now,

00:34:42.300 --> 00:34:46.080
see how dramatic that is with
a dark side and a light side?

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:48.600
So just, now look at me.

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:51.100
Look at how that
dramatically changes.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:55.739
So in just this small area,
moving the camera a little bit,

00:34:55.739 --> 00:34:58.080
moving the subject
a little bit, we

00:34:58.080 --> 00:35:01.075
can adjust the light
for what works.

00:35:01.075 --> 00:35:02.700
CHRIS BOEBEL: And it
ultimately depends

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:05.550
on determining what
works for your scene.

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:06.810
DAVID TAMES: Exactly.

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:10.140
And if we need fill, we
could bring in the Flexfill

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:11.100
if necessary.

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:14.790
But typically, when I'm shooting
like this I might be solo lobo,

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:17.370
and there's no one
to hold a reflector.

00:35:17.370 --> 00:35:19.740
So if I can find
a nice position,

00:35:19.740 --> 00:35:24.060
a nice, soft window light
somewhere, hopefully

00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:25.102
away from a lot of noise.

00:35:25.102 --> 00:35:27.435
CHRIS BOEBEL: So let's go
back to the dramatic position,

00:35:27.435 --> 00:35:29.205
though, maybe Zack
can hold the Flexfill

00:35:29.205 --> 00:35:30.705
and put it in and
just you can see--

00:35:30.705 --> 00:35:30.810
DAVID TAMES: Yeah.

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:31.518
Or I can do that.

00:35:33.512 --> 00:35:35.470
So here, come away from
the window a little bit

00:35:35.470 --> 00:35:36.540
so it's even more dramatic.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:37.040
Right?

00:35:40.610 --> 00:35:41.840
Pretty dramatic.

00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:44.390
So that's a pretty
dramatic, that's

00:35:44.390 --> 00:35:46.040
a pretty intense lighting ratio.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:54.740
But notice, using the Flexfill,
I can fill in the shadows.

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:56.660
Wax off.

00:35:56.660 --> 00:35:59.010
Wax on.

00:35:59.010 --> 00:35:59.525
Wax off.

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:03.720
Wax on.

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:08.760
If I use the matte white side,
the effect is more subtle.

00:36:08.760 --> 00:36:10.860
Wax off.

00:36:10.860 --> 00:36:13.030
Wax on.

00:36:13.030 --> 00:36:19.090
So the Flexfill can provide you
with really nice fill light.

00:36:19.090 --> 00:36:21.430
So typically, when
we're working outdoors

00:36:21.430 --> 00:36:26.080
and we're going low tech,
Flexfill and Mother Nature.

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:28.510
Cinema is light moving in time.

00:36:32.182 --> 00:36:34.630
CHRIS BOEBEL: [LAUGHS]
That's your wrap up?

00:36:34.630 --> 00:36:36.430
DAVID TAMES: Yeah.

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:38.830
CHRIS BOEBEL: So I'll
just say that we've

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:42.280
looked at a lot of tools
today and everything

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:47.440
from clothespins, C-47s,
to Fresnel lights and more.

00:36:47.440 --> 00:36:51.250
But ultimately, the most
important tool is your eye.

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:53.800
And that's what I was trying
to get at earlier when I talked

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:56.290
about actually seeing
what's in the scene

00:36:56.290 --> 00:36:59.140
and making changes based
on what you're seeing.

00:36:59.140 --> 00:37:00.820
Your eye is an
incredible instrument.

00:37:00.820 --> 00:37:02.500
It's far better than any camera.

00:37:02.500 --> 00:37:05.080
It's far more adaptable in
different lighting situations.

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:07.330
In fact, it's so good that
you have to sometimes learn

00:37:07.330 --> 00:37:09.940
to see past it and see
what's actually there

00:37:09.940 --> 00:37:12.580
and what a camera would see,
which is not as adaptable.

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:16.120
So it takes a lot of
practice until it eventually

00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:17.410
becomes second nature.

00:37:17.410 --> 00:37:21.250
But taking a look at the scene
and then making adjustments

00:37:21.250 --> 00:37:24.490
either to your subject
or to the light,

00:37:24.490 --> 00:37:27.640
whether they be lighting
instruments or windows,

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:30.520
is really the basic
process of doing lighting,

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:32.920
as it is the basic
process of almost

00:37:32.920 --> 00:37:34.493
any aspect of filmmaking.

00:37:34.493 --> 00:37:35.410
So thanks a lot, Dave.

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:36.700
I appreciate you coming in.

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:37.450
DAVID TAMES: Happy to come in.

00:37:37.450 --> 00:37:38.450
CHRIS BOEBEL: Fantastic.

00:37:38.450 --> 00:37:38.950
And--

00:37:38.950 --> 00:37:39.950
DAVID TAMES: Lot of fun.

00:37:39.950 --> 00:37:42.130
CHRIS BOEBEL: --your blog
Kino-Eye.com is a great

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:46.392
resource for not just lighting
tips, but video and media--

00:37:46.392 --> 00:37:47.725
DAVID TAMES: Documentary mostly.

00:37:47.725 --> 00:37:48.760
CHRIS BOEBEL:
--tips, documentary.

00:37:48.760 --> 00:37:49.720
DAVID TAMES: And
I'd like to thank

00:37:49.720 --> 00:37:50.970
Zack Ray for helping us today.

00:37:50.970 --> 00:37:52.428
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
thanks again also

00:37:52.428 --> 00:37:54.490
to our terrific cameraman,
Jared [? Juniak, ?]

00:37:54.490 --> 00:38:01.440
who is living what we're just
talking about as we film this.

